[Music]  you know i was thinking about this song  i heard on the radio the other day sam  this scene is one of the most powerful  scenes in our film  how many roads must  walk down before you call  him a man the bob dylan blowing in the  wind  was a nagging thing to him because he  did feel like i should have written that  song i just  love those lyrics this debate between  sam and malcolm  is really this this uh the undercurrent  for the whole plot how  many roads must have that little subtle  embarrassment that  leslie is playing is great she's always  asking how much do the oppressed  have to do before they can be recognized  as human  beings it's one of those songs that  speaks to  injustice that speaks to hope on those  are timeless  ideals that relate to every generation  this is a white boy  from minnesota who has nothing to gain  from writing a song that speaks more to  the struggles of our people more  to the movement than anything that you  have ever penned in your  life all of them were so present  in this moment and this moment just  breaks my  heart why has this song  gone higher on the pop charts  than anything you got out this was a  difficult scene to shoot because it was  so dialogue heavy and the amount of  emotion that kingsley had to give up  each time just blows me away  good riddance regina is unintrusive  she's let me have full reign in my  sort of crazy process and method there  is no  more room for anyone  not you not me not jimmy not sam  no one to be standing on the fence  anymore  this film works up to this moment and  when it comes it hits you like a freight  train  our people are literally dying in the  streets every day  black people are dying  everyday it's why i love being an actor  i'm getting emotional  we're talking about four of the probably  most  iconic men in american history but for a  lot of people in the world  oh my goodness oh my so pretty  we all kind of looked at them as deities  forgetting to look at them as men first  what originally sparked me to want to  write about this story is that it was a  real night  february 25th 1964 it was really  interesting to think about these  four gentlemen from very different areas  and in this very specific segregated  time  i thought of all four of them as just so  unapologetically black  yes catches marcellus clay is the new  heavyweight  champion of the world the dialogue just  punched me in the gut the things that  were  most important to me that i wanted to  push further  were the vulnerability and strength that  black men have  that the men that i know possess i  wanted the  heart of the film to be about having  these four men have a private discussion  that we get to be privy to  unfortunately there wasn't a lot of  information about what they actually  discussed  i decided what if i took everything i  knew about what happened leading up to  that night and use it to  inform and power the conversation that  these four men would have  you used to be such a fun cat now you  acting in private the way you are on  camera  i think what happened is the occasion  turned into an opportunity  for these four men to get together and  discuss things there were very few  people  living in america who would understand  the life that they were living  who would understand the unique  pressures they were under it's a work of  fiction  but i like to say that it's a work of  fiction powered by fact because the  reality of it is that 1964 was a  crucible year for all four of these men  and they all experienced transformative  change  in the days weeks and months after this  particular night  i thought that this film would work best  if it was a director who was a fresh  voice one of the first people who got to  read the script was regina  after i got the job i said i feel like  this is a love letter to black men  it's so honest it's so raw  it's so quiet it's so loud  everything's not so black and white like  you make it out to be well this is all  to a hopping star  balancing the levity of the subject  matter of the conversations  a lot of that came from just having a  great quartet  of actors where they truly understood  that they were brothers this movement  that we are in  is called a struggle because we are  fighting for our lives  this is a film about respect and love  and we're showing men  who are supporting each other in a way  that is not often celebrated  brothers like sam and you and cassius  you all are our greatest weapons not  anyone's weapons  malcolm you need to be jimmy the debate  that these guys engage in is a debate  that's been going on since long before  them and it's a debate that's still  going on today  how do we progress as a people how do we  bring about change  that's the debate the line has got to be  drawn in the sand jimmy  a line that says either you stand on  this side with us  or you stand over on that side against  us cast and i i believe  i believe in that brother's potential  jimmy too much  too much to let him stay over on the  other side all four of  the men in this film have impacted our  lives in some way even if we didn't know  that our lives were being impacted by  them the goal is for us to  really be free  [Music]  we want the world to speak but we're  safe to be ourselves  [Music]  without having to answer to anybody for  it  we have to be there for each other  brothers who could move mountains  without lifting a finger

